!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
Order is data
description: one ordered container — positions are integer keys, and only the container writes them
- |
  There is no separate list type. A mapping is **ordered**, and its positions are integer
  keys: a keyless entry (a `- item` sequence element) takes only its position; a keyed
  entry holds a position *too* — its position is a `*`-alias to it. A "list" is just a
  mapping whose keys are 0, 1, 2, …, and access keeps the two axes apart: `[n]` selects
  the integer key (position), `: x` selects the string key.
- !!yo
  key0: value0     #  ≡   key0: value0
  - value1         #      0: *key0      position 0 aliases the keyed entry
  key2: value2     #      1: value1     the keyless entry lives at its key
                   #      key2: value2
                   #      2: *key2
- |
  Order is **data**, never derived. In a file it is text order; for a directory the
  `.yo/body.yo` overlay imposes it — a pointer array whose element `- *file` consumes the
  pointer and grants the named child its position — on the subset it names. A child the
  body never names stays a keyed-only member after the ordered block; it is never granted
  an invented position.
- |
  Only the container writes its own order. A remote node may declare *that* a container
  holds it (an ordinal anchor `&: path[]`), never *where*: `&: path[3]` is rejected,
  because a position claim would be a second writer for single-writer data — any
  insertion in the container silently invalidates the claim, and two members can claim
  one slot. If an exact position matters, author it forward, in the container itself.
